for more on john lewis we're joined now  by the chairwoman of the congressional  black caucus  congresswoman karen bass of california  congresswoman good morning  john lewis's passing marks the end of an  era the last of the big six leaders who  planned the march on washington he  wasn't just a civil rights icon but a  multi-generational leader  and legend how do you begin to describe  his legacy  well i will tell you that i have had the  honor for the last 10 years  of serving with him and coming in the  congress and being able to sit in a room  with  a giant like mr lewis and just in my  time  alone i watch tim one make sure that we  have a museum  the african-american history museum i  watched him build a monument to the  enslaved americans  that built the capitol he had  so much influence and was responsible  for  so much incredible change in our country  that his memory will live on but you  know what  on the day of his passing we need to all  recommit to make sure that we continue  to get in good trouble  that we continue to speak out speak up  and  make sure especially today when so much  change is happening it's really our  responsibility to carry his legacy on  now what what i wonder what what he  personally taught you  about how to affect change in washington  i think what he taught me more than  anything was patience  his calm demeanor the fact that everyone  in the house  democrats republicans everyone respected  him  i remember one time when one of my  colleagues on the other side of the  aisle  introduced an amendment that was going  to cut funding  around voting rights around enforcement  of voting rights and mr lewis saw it on  tv from his office  he ran down to the floor he gave one of  those  incredible speeches and my colleague on  the other side of the aisle  apologized in public and withdrew that  amendment  that was the power that he had in the  house of representatives  and i had the honor on the 50th  anniversary to walk across that bridge  and i hope to be there again when that  bridge is renamed for him  the nation is seeing what what it looks  like this morning without without john  lewis  but congresswoman have you thought about  what what congress will look like  without john lewis well uh and you know  what we're in session this week  and it'll be a sad week i'm sure that  many of us will be on the floor paying  tribute to mr lewis  that was one thing he used to always say  to me he tried to get me to call him  john  and i told him i said no mr lewis i'm  never calling you john  you're mr lewis to me congresswoman it's  dana jacobson now we were talking with  ambassador  young earlier and he commented on that  loving spirit that really dominated  uh congressman lewis despite all that he  had been through how did you see that  spirit  play out time and time again well i  think we all did and no matter what was  going on in the house  no matter how divisive  it became there was always mr lewis that  would just have that moral authority  that would allow us to uh rise up  above our partisan differences and uh  and i think that's part of his legacy i  think he taught us  how you should go about creating change  when you have differences certainly a  message that a lot of people can look to  now congresswoman karen bass  absolutely thank you very much
 

